@@ -10539,6 +10539,60 @@ source API. Popper pass-through positioning options remain accepted wire keys wi
 anchoring as an accepted difference. The sanitizer allowlist and sanitizer overrides are J-ENGINE's,
 and the Tooltip row names the `Sanitizer` and `TemplateFactory` utilities that carry them.
 
+## Hosts
+
+Veneer's Node and npm floors are the `engines.node` and `devEngines.packageManager` fields of the
+`package.json` file; this section names those fields and doesn't restate them. npm 11.19.1 refuses
+to run a script under an npm version the `devEngines.packageManager` field excludes, and runs one
+under a Node version the `engines.node` field excludes, so the guides proof holds each receipt's
+Node version against that floor. Veneer's browser families are Chromium, Chrome, and Edge. Veneer
+ships no host check and selects no browser: the browser projects and the distribution proof launch
+the browser the `PLAYWRIGHT_EXECUTABLE_PATH`, `PLAYWRIGHT_WS_ENDPOINT`, or `PLAYWRIGHT_CHANNEL`
+variable names. Otherwise the resolver in the `configs/browsers.ts` file takes the first it finds of
+the Chromium Playwright manages, a Chromium the host bundles, the installed `chrome` channel, and
+the installed `msedge` channel. Where it finds none, it names the platform's default channel
+without checking that the channel is installed: `msedge` on Windows and `chrome` on every other
+platform.
+
+A supported host is a Playwright channel on a platform, and "every supported host" means every row
+of the Supported hosts table with a passing receipt in the Receipts table. A receipt records one run
+of package scripts on one host and the Veneer revision it measured. A sentence naming a browser
+build must rest on a receipt; the ER-PROSE unit owns the gate that holds each such sentence to one.
+The Channel, Build, Platform, Kernel, Node, and npm cells hold what the `readRuntime` reader in
+[the server setup module](../tests/setupServer.ts) returns on that host, so the build is the one the
+browser reports rather than the major its user agent names. An Owner cell names the unit that takes
+the host's first passing receipt, and reads `—` after that receipt is recorded. The `chrome` row
+names no platform until its owner records the platform the maintainer chooses.
+
+The readers of both tables refuse a malformed cell and name its row, including a Platform cell that
+holds no value Node reports as its platform (a Supported hosts row may write `—` for a platform the
+guide doesn't name yet) and a Commands cell whose code spans are not separated by commas. The guides
+proof refuses a receipt naming a host outside the Supported hosts
+table, a host with a passing receipt that still names an owner, a host with neither an owner nor a
+passing receipt, a receipt whose Node or npm version is under its `package.json` floor, a command
+naming no `package.json` script, and a repeated row; see
+[the host record gates](../tests/guides.test.ts). When the distribution project runs in release
+mode, its release-host case launches the browser the browser projects launch and fails unless a
+passing receipt names that run's channel, platform, and build. In any other mode the case passes
+when a passing receipt matches and skips when none does. When no passing receipt matches, its
+message names every host value the missing row needs (channel, build, platform, kernel, Node, and npm) and says
+the Date, Revision, and Commands cells come from the run that writes the row; see
+[the release-host case](../tests/distribution.test.ts).
+
+### Supported hosts
+
+| Channel    | Platform | Owner     |
+| ---------- | -------- | --------- |
+| `chromium` | `linux`  | ER-LINUX  |
+| `chromium` | `win32`  | ER-WIN    |
+| `msedge`   | `win32`  | ER-WIN    |
+| `chrome`   | —        | ER-CHROME |
+
+### Receipts
+
+| Date | Revision | Channel | Build | Platform | Kernel | Node | npm | Commands | Result |
+| ---- | -------- | ------- | ----- | -------- | ------ | ---- | --- | -------- | ------ |
+
